// Auto-injected standard library. See internal/prelude/prelude.go
// for the rationale; in short, helpers here go through the IR like
// any user code, so they get peephole / dce / inline optimisation
// and drop into every backend without backend-specific shims.
//
// This file is now JUST an import list. Every helper has migrated
// into a `std/…` or `core/…` module — `injectPrelude` follows
// the imports below via `modload.LoadStdlibFlatSkipping` and
// inlines their bodies under the bare-name namespace user code
// expects (`abs(...)`, `format(...)`, `int_to_string(...)`, etc.).
//
// User code can shadow / override anything by declaring a same-
// named function — useful for testing or for callers that need
// different semantics (eg a stricter `s.parse_int()` that errors
// on whitespace).
//
// The injected stdlib decls dedupe against
// `ast.Program.LoadedStdlibPaths` so a user program that explicitly
// `import`s the same stdlib module via modload doesn't get a
// "method redeclared" error.
//
// Phase 5 of docs/PRELUDE-TO-MODULES.md flips the switch:
// programs opt into `import "core/no_prelude";` today; once every
// e2e + example program migrates to explicit imports the file
// (and `injectPrelude` itself) goes away entirely.

import "std/i32";
import "std/i64";
import "std/u32";
import "std/u64";
import "std/string";
import "std/array";
import "std/log";
import "std/sort";
import "std/csv";
import "std/format";
import "std/http";
import "std/io";
import "std/path";
import "std/base64";
import "std/hex";
import "std/url";
import "std/json";
import "std/math";
import "std/float";
import "std/tcp";
import "std/test";
import "std/fuzz";
import "std/time";
import "core/int";
import "core/map";
